# Copy to .env and set only what you need. Never commit .env — it holds secrets.
#
# Every variable is optional: a fresh install starts with none of them set. The app reads the
# variables that start with MAM_, and a double underscore separates configuration sections, so
# MAM_Storage__DataDirectory sets Storage:DataDirectory.
#
# Docker Compose reads the .env next to docker-compose.yml twice: it fills in the APP_* variables of
# the compose file, and it passes every variable here into the container, where the app picks up the
# MAM_ ones. When you run the app from source instead, export the variables in your shell, or use
# .NET User Secrets for local development (see README.md, "Configuration").

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Docker Compose — OPTIONAL, added in Phase A (MAM-3)
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Read by docker-compose.yml, not by the app.

# Which image to run: latest (the newest release), a release such as 0.1.0, or a minor version
# such as 0.1 (its newest patch). Default: latest.
# Trade-off: latest moves to every new release, minor and major ones included, when you run
# `bash deploy.sh`. Pin a release number to update only when you choose to.
# APP_TAG=latest

# The host address the port is published on. Default: 127.0.0.1, so only this machine can open the
# app.
# Trade-off: 0.0.0.0 shows the app to everyone who can reach this host. Create the owner account
# first: until it exists, whoever opens the app first becomes the owner. The app speaks plain HTTP,
# so passwords and tokens cross the network unencrypted; open it only on a network you trust, or
# keep 127.0.0.1 and put a reverse proxy that adds HTTPS on this host. The app then sees every client
# as coming from one address, so they share the limit of 10 sign-in attempts a minute, and the
# refresh cookie isn't marked Secure (README.md, "Security").
# APP_BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1

# The host port. Default: 8118, the port the app listens on inside the container too.
# APP_PORT=8118

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Storage — OPTIONAL, added in Phase A (MAM-1)
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Directory for the SQLite database (mam.db) and the .secrets directory. A relative path resolves
# against the API's content root. Default: Data — leave the line commented out to keep it.
# Trade-off: keep it on a local disk. SQLite runs in WAL mode, which does not work reliably on a
# network filesystem (NFS, SMB), and a corrupted database takes every job and setting with it.
# Docker: ignored. The compose file pins it to /data, where the mam-data volume is mounted, so a
# value copied here from a local run can't move the database off the volume.
# MAM_Storage__DataDirectory=Data

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Secrets — OPTIONAL, added in Phase A (MAM-1)
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Blank = auto-generate. On the first start the app generates each blank secret into
# <data directory>/.secrets (directory 0700, files 0600 on Linux and macOS), reuses it on every
# later start and never overwrites it. A value set here always wins, and then no file is written.
# Back up .secrets together with mam.db.

# Key that encrypts stored integration credentials with AES-256-GCM: the Jellyfin, indexer and
# qBittorrent passwords and API keys, from MAM-7 on. Nothing uses it yet.
# Base64 of exactly 32 random bytes:  openssl rand -base64 32
# Trade-off: lose or change this key and every stored credential becomes unreadable — you would
# have to enter them all again. Setting it here keeps the key out of the data directory (useful
# when backups of that directory are less protected than your environment), but then keeping it is
# your job: if the variable disappears, the app generates a new key and the old credentials are
# lost.
MAM_Encryption__Key=

# Secret that signs the 15-minute access tokens of sign-in (MAM-4).
# Base64 of 64 random bytes (openssl wraps long output, hence the tr):
#   openssl rand -base64 64 | tr -d '\n'
# Fewer than 32 bytes is refused: sign-in fails, and the log names this variable.
# Trade-off: changing it signs nobody out. The access tokens already issued stop validating, and
# open tabs quietly get new ones through their refresh cookie, which this secret doesn't sign;
# nothing else is lost. Rotate it when you suspect it leaked. To lock someone out, disable them or
# reset their password on the Users page instead: either ends their sessions at once.
MAM_Jwt__Secret=

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  ASP.NET Core — OPTIONAL, added in Phase A (MAM-1)
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Addresses the API listens on when you run the published app. `dotnet run` overrides it with
# http://localhost:5080 from Properties/launchSettings.json.
# Trade-off: listening on anything but localhost (for example http://0.0.0.0:5080) shows the app to
# everyone who can reach the port, over plain HTTP. Create the owner account first: until it exists,
# whoever opens the app first becomes the owner.
# Docker: leave it unset. The image listens on 8118 and its health check probes that port; choose
# the host side with APP_PORT and APP_BIND_ADDRESS instead.
# ASPNETCORE_URLS=http://localhost:5080
